<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Schlei</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Schlei is a long, narrow inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, reaching about 40 kilometres inland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a> stood near its western end, and the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>, later capital of the Duchy of Schleswig, grew up on its shore. Its sheltered waters linked Baltic trade to the short overland route towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/schlei.jpg" alt="Schlei" width="320" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EstherJoh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SchleiSlien.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
